{"parentUuid":"65c2489d-0527-47c4-9baa-5443c1fa515d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"dc745141-e049-4a40-b8ba-0fe812cbba1f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/zenray/Library/Application Support/Claude/local-agent-mode-sessions/skills-plugin/e410da62-b0cb-4839-aece-40580ca951ac/a28d2458-e55b-4cf3-acec-322cb0778c40/skills/kanji-stroke-order\n\n# Kanji Stroke Order Diagram\n\nGenerates a self-contained SVG showing how to write a Japanese character, one stroke at a time, in the canonical order published by KanjiVG (the de facto reference dataset, used by Tagaini Jisho, jisho.org, and most kanji-learning apps).\n\n## When to use\n\nThe user gives a single Japanese character (kanji, hiragana, or katakana) and the conversation is about writing it, learning it, memorizing it, or its stroke composition. Examples:\n\n- \"How do I write 字?\"\n- \"Stroke order for あ\"\n- \"Show me the strokes of 語 / 書き順 / kakijun\"\n- \"I keep forgetting the order, give me a chart for 漢字\"\n- \"Decompose 語 stroke by stroke\"\n\nIf the user provides multiple characters in one request, run the script once per character and present the diagrams together.\n\n## How to run\n\nThe script is at `scripts/stroke_diagram.py` and uses only the Python standard library plus an internet connection (it fetches the SVG from the KanjiVG GitHub raw endpoint).\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/stroke_diagram.py <character> -o /mnt/user-data/outputs/<character>_strokes.svg\n```\n\nOptional flags:\n- `--cols N` — max panels per row before wrapping (default 6). For very simple characters (3-4 strokes), pass `--cols 3` or `--cols 4` to keep panels large.\n\nAlways write the output to `/mnt/user-data/outputs/` so the file can be presented to the user. After generation, call `present_files` with the SVG path so the user can open and download it.\n\n## Output format\n\nA single SVG file. One panel per stroke (109×109 each), laid out left-to-right with automatic row wrapping past 6 panels. In each panel:\n\n- Completed strokes: solid black\n- Current stroke: solid black, slightly thicker\n- Future strokes: light gray (so the final character outline is visible from the first panel, giving the learner a target)\n- Red label at the stroke's starting point: stroke number + arrow (→ ← ↑ ↓) indicating the initial direction of the brush\n\nThe SVG renders correctly in any modern browser. PNG conversion via cairosvg or ImageMagick is possible but the arrows and the Japanese title glyph may fail to render without a CJK-capable font installed on the conversion host. The SVG itself is always correct.\n\n## Coverage and failure mode\n\nKanjiVG covers:\n- All jōyō kanji (2,136)\n- All jinmeiyō kanji (~860)\n- Hiragana and katakana (full sets)\n- Many additional CJK Unified Ideographs\n\nRare characters from CJK Extensions B–G may not be present. In that case the script exits with a clear error citing the U+ codepoint that was missed; suggest the user try a more common synonym or check that the character is correct.\n\n## Attribution\n\nKanjiVG data is © Ulrich Apel and contributors, licensed CC BY-SA 3.0. When generating diagrams for public redistribution (blog posts, published materials), credit KanjiVG and link to https://kanjivg.tagaini.net/.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: 何 道 海 — génère un SVG textbook par kanji (strokes complets en noir, stroke courant en rouge avec numéro et flèche au point de départ, strokes restants en gris clair). Sortie : 3 SVG séparés, un par kanji. Je les intégrerai ensuite dans 3 fichiers HTML enrichis avec ON/KUN/mnémo/exemples/phrase, sauvegardés dans /Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory/Japanese/KanjiProgress/001-100 N5/ sous les noms \"059 何 Nani Nan.html\", \"060 道 Michi Dou.html\", \"061 海 Umi Kai.html\"."}]},"isMeta":true,"uuid":"94409d3c-4b6a-4491-b959-dbc0c4a50be4","timestamp":"2026-05-28T08:13:05.533Z","sourceToolUseID":"toolu_01G8gjFdmiVfSHDMAZqfgxpz","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","